---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Weather API Demo | Mike Kelley"
  description="An interactive weather lookup demonstrating API integration, input validation, asynchronous requests, accessible feedback, and technical communication."
>
  <section class="page-hero wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">CODE DEMO / API INTEGRATION</p>
    <h1>A small working app, with the decisions explained.</h1>
    <p class="lede">I built this weather lookup to demonstrate the full path from user input to an external API response, then documented how the pieces fit together. The application is intentionally small. The focus is on clear behavior, readable implementation, and explaining technical choices.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href="#try-it">Try the demo</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/MikeKelley89/openWeatherApi" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View source on GitHub ↗</a>
    </div>
    <div class="proof-row">
      <div><strong>JavaScript + jQuery</strong><span>Form handling and asynchronous requests</span></div>
      <div><strong>OpenWeather APIs</strong><span>Geocoding and current conditions</span></div>
      <div><strong>Written by me</strong><span>Application, interface, and walkthrough</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band" id="try-it">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">LIVE DEMONSTRATION</p>
        <h2>Look up weather by US ZIP code</h2>
        <p>Enter a five-digit US ZIP code. The app resolves it to coordinates, requests current conditions and the daily summary, and displays the first alert if the API returns one.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="weather-form">
        <form id="localWeather">
          <label for="zip">US ZIP code</label>
          <div class="input-row">
            <input id="zip" name="zip" type="text" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="postal-code" pattern="[0-9]{5}" maxlength="5" placeholder="e.g. 84010" aria-describedby="zipHelp zipStatus" required />
            <button id="findWeather" class="button primary" type="submit" disabled>Check weather</button>
          </div>
          <p class="form-help" id="zipHelp">Use five digits. ZIP+4 is not supported.</p>
          <p class="form-help error-text" id="zipStatus" hidden>Enter a valid five-digit US ZIP code.</p>
        </form>
        <p class="weather-status" id="weatherStatus" role="status" aria-live="polite">Enter a ZIP code to look up local conditions.</p>

        <div class="weather-result" id="weatherError" role="alert" hidden>
          <strong>Weather lookup failed</strong>
          <p><span id="errorCode"></span><span id="errorMsg"></span></p>
        </div>

        <div class="weather-result" id="weatherAlert" role="alert" hidden>
          <p class="eyebrow">WEATHER ALERT</p>
          <h3 id="event">Weather alert</h3>
          <p><strong>Issued by:</strong> <span id="sender_name"></span></p>
          <p><strong>Starts:</strong> <span id="start"></span></p>
          <p><strong>Ends:</strong> <span id="end"></span></p>
          <p><strong>Description:</strong> <span id="description"></span></p>
          <p><strong>Type:</strong> <span id="tags"></span></p>
        </div>

        <div class="weather-result" id="weatherResults" hidden>
          <h3 id="conditions">Current conditions</h3>
          <div class="weather-main">
            <div>
              <div class="weather-temp"><span id="main_temp">--</span><span aria-label="degrees Fahrenheit">°F</span></div>
              <p>Current temperature</p>
            </div>
            <div class="weather-meta">
              <span>Wind speed<strong><span id="wind_speed">--</span> mph</strong></span>
              <span>Wind direction<strong><span id="wind_direction">--</span>°</strong></span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p id="weather_conditions"></p>
        </div>
        <p class="small-note">This is a portfolio demonstration, not an emergency weather service. The browser calls a Cloudflare Worker; the OpenWeather key is stored as a Worker secret and is not included in client-side code. The public endpoint still needs provider-side usage limits and monitoring.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">UNDER THE HOOD</p>
      <h2>From ZIP code to weather data</h2>
      <p>The app makes two requests because the weather endpoint needs coordinates, while most people know their ZIP code rather than their latitude and longitude.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pipeline">
      <article><span>01</span><h3>Validate input</h3><p>Check that the value contains exactly five digits before starting the request flow.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><h3>Resolve location</h3><p>Send the ZIP code to OpenWeather's Geocoding API to get a location name and coordinates.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><h3>Request conditions</h3><p>Pass latitude and longitude to One Call API 3.0, requesting imperial units and excluding unused data.</p></article>
      <article><span>04</span><h3>Present the response</h3><p>Update the page with temperature, wind, a daily summary, and the first available alert.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">REQUEST ARCHITECTURE</p>
      <h2>The credential stays on the server.</h2>
      <p>The browser sends a ZIP code to a narrowly scoped Cloudflare Worker. The Worker validates the request, checks a rate limit, calls OpenWeather with its secret, and returns only the location and weather fields the interface needs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="architecture-flow" role="img" aria-label="Browser sends ZIP code to Cloudflare Worker. Worker validates and rate limits the request, calls OpenWeather Geocoding and One Call APIs using a server-side secret, then returns weather data for display.">
      <article class="architecture-node"><span>01</span><strong>Browser</strong><p>Validate ZIP and request <code>/weather?zip=84010</code>.</p></article>
      <div class="architecture-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <article class="architecture-node"><span>02</span><strong>Cloudflare Worker</strong><p>Validate origin, method, input, and request rate.</p></article>
      <div class="architecture-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <article class="architecture-node"><span>03</span><strong>OpenWeather</strong><p>Resolve coordinates, retrieve weather, return a reduced response.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="small-note">The <code>OPENWEATHER_API_KEY</code> secret is available only to the Worker runtime. CORS is not authentication, and Cloudflare's rate limit is approximate, so provider-side usage limits remain important.</p>
    <p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/MikeKelley89/openWeatherApi/tree/main/worker" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the Worker implementation and deployment guide ↗</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">IMPLEMENTATION CHOICES</p>
        <h2>What this example demonstrates</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature-card"><span class="feature-number">01 / INPUT</span><h3>Validation before submission</h3><p>The interface checks the ZIP format and disables the submit button until the input is valid. Format validation does not guarantee that a ZIP code exists.</p></article>
        <article class="feature-card"><span class="feature-number">02 / ASYNC</span><h3>Loading and error feedback</h3><p>The interface tells the user when a lookup is in progress and distinguishes failed requests from returned weather data.</p></article>
        <article class="feature-card"><span class="feature-number">03 / ACCESSIBILITY</span><h3>Clear, perceivable updates</h3><p>The form has a visible label, the status uses a live region, and errors and results are revealed in the page rather than relying on color alone.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">TECHNICAL COMMUNICATION</p>
        <h2>The explanation is part of the project.</h2>
        <p>I also wrote a walkthrough that explains the API flow, key code paths, trade-offs, and limitations. It was written in an informal blog style rather than as formal product documentation, but the goal is the same: help a reader understand what the system does and why.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href="https://mikekelley89.github.io/openWeatherApi/demos/openweather.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the original code walkthrough ↗</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="code-panel">
        <pre><code>// 1. Resolve a ZIP code to coordinates
GET /geo/1.0/zip

// 2. Request weather for those coordinates
GET /data/3.0/onecall

// 3. Present the response
current conditions + daily summary + alerts</code></pre>
        <p class="small-note">Simplified request flow, not executable code.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">SCOPE AND LIMITATIONS</p>
        <h2>A demonstration, not a production weather product</h2>
        <p>This project is intentionally small. A production version would need additional work, and identifying that work is part of the engineering judgment this sample is meant to show.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="check-grid">
        <p><strong>Secret management</strong><br />The Worker keeps the provider key out of browser code. The public endpoint still needs monitoring, usage limits, and abuse controls.</p>
        <p><strong>Real input validation</strong><br />Check whether a ZIP code resolves successfully, not only whether it matches five digits.</p>
        <p><strong>Complete alert handling</strong><br />Iterate through all returned alerts instead of displaying only the first one.</p>
        <p><strong>More resilient states</strong><br />Add tests and clearer handling for empty responses, rate limits, timeouts, and partial API data.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="small-note" style="margin-top:26px">I chose a limited scope to keep the example focused. These are known areas for improvement, not claims that the app is production-ready.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="contact-strip">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">MORE FROM MY PORTFOLIO</p>
        <h2>Technical writing, implementation, and the space between them.</h2>
        <p>My work spans product documentation, API and software documentation, docs-as-code workflows, and hands-on front-end examples.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}writing/`}>View writing samples</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href={base}>Back to home</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script is:inline src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.1.min.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="/scripts/weather-demo.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>
